Troubleshooting:
      I practiced some basic commands that can help identify common Linux and application problems.

1. Check if a Service is Running
   Command:
          systemctl status <service>
   What it does:
          Shows the current status of a service.
   What I learned:
          I can use this as one of the first checks when a service is not working.

2. Check Running Processes
   Command:
          ps -ef | grep <process>
   What it does:
          Searches for a particular process in the list of running processes.
   What I learned:
          It helps me check whether an application or process is running.

3. Check Port
   Command:
          ss -lntp | grep 8080
   What it does:
          Checks whether something is listening on port 8080.
   What I learned:
          If an application is expected to use port 8080, this command can help me check whether the port is listening.

4. Test Local Application
   Command:
          curl localhost:8080
   What it does:
          Sends a request to an application running on port 8080 on the local machine.
   What I learned:
          It can help me check whether a local web application is responding.

5. Check Disk Space
   Command:
          df -h
   What it does:
          Shows the available and used disk space.
   What I learned:
          Low disk space can cause applications and services to behave unexpectedly, so I can check disk usage during troubleshooting.

6. Check Memory
  Command:
          free -h
  What it does:
          Shows RAM and swap usage.
  What I learned:
          I can check memory usage when an application is slow or a system is running out of memory.

7. Check Logs
   Command:
          journalctl -u <service>
   What it does:
          Shows logs for a specific service.
   What I learned:
          Logs can provide useful information about errors or problems with a service.

8. Check Recent Log Entries
   Command:
          tail -n 20 /var/log/syslog
   What it does:
          Shows the last 20 lines of the system log.
   What I learned:
          I can use recent log entries to look for errors or events that happened recently.

9. Check Network Connectivity
   Command:
          ping 8.8.8.8
   What it does:
          Tests connectivity to a public IP address.
   What I learned:
          It helps me check whether my system can reach an external network address.

10. Check DNS
    Command:
            nslookup google.com
    What it does:
            Queries DNS to find the IP address associated with a domain name.
    What I learned:
            It helps me check whether DNS name resolution is working.

Basic Troubleshooting Approach: 

When an application or service is not working, I can start with simple checks:
      1.Check whether the service is running.
      2.Check whether the process is running.
      3.Check whether the expected port is listening.
      4.Check the application or system logs.
      5.Check CPU, memory, and disk usage.
      6.Check network connectivity.
      7.Check DNS if the problem involves a hostname.
